  "labeling": "Hand-labeled by reading the 300 DPI render. SIX OF ELEVEN COLUMNS ARE LABELED: the five that carry the page's row arithmetic -- U.S., Alaska, Puerto Rico, Other, Continental United States -- plus Connecticut, the RIGHTMOST column on the page. The six state columns in between (Alabama, Arizona, Arkansas, California, Colorado) are deliberately not labeled; they repeat an extraction problem the labeled columns already pose and would have made this one page most of the corpus by value count. Connecticut is labeled because a parser that truncates the right edge of a landscape table, or slips by one column, is otherwise not penalized at all -- it reports Colorado's figures under Connecticut and every check still passes.\n\nTWO DIRECTIONS OF ARITHMETIC, AND THEY ARE NOT EQUALLY STRONG. Across each row, U.S. = Alaska + Puerto Rico + Other + Continental U.S. All 33 of those close TO THE UNIT, so every value in the five leading columns is verified rather than merely careful. Down each column, the components do NOT add exactly to the printed total: amounts are printed in THOUSANDS of dollars and each is rounded on its own, so 19 of the 36 column sums miss by 1 to 4. Those carry tolerance 5 and are a weaker check -- they cannot see an error smaller than 5 -- which is stated here so no write-up claims otherwise. Connecticut takes part in no exact identity; its 33 values are checked only by its 6 tolerance sums.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC RESOLVED FOUR AMBIGUOUS GLYPHS. This scan confuses 3 and 8 at the top of the digit: Alaska's losses 'All other' (35, not 85), the Other column's salaries and wages (553, not 558), Puerto Rico's losses and charge-offs on loans (23, not 28) and the Other column's transfers to reserve accounts on securities (37, not 87) were each read wrong first and each caught by the row identity failing. The embedded OCR layer lost the 35 entirely. Amounts are labeled as printed, in thousands, without a dollar sign.\n\nEVERY ROW CARRIES AN EXPLICIT key BECAUSE THE PAGE REPEATS ITS LABELS. 'Recoveries', 'Transfers from reserve accounts', 'Transfers to reserve accounts', 'Losses and charge-offs' and 'All other' each appear twice, once under 'On securities:' and once under 'On loans:'. The anchor stays the printed text, which is what score.py matches against parser output; the key is what the 36 sums name. Several anchors are FRAGMENTS of a label that the page wraps across two lines, chosen to be the part that sits on one printed line.",
